==Status Report: 2026-10-10 23:31 PDT==
ANALYSIS ONLY (no code changed, per the ticket's request).

Short answer: the "player in the drawer" (the media notification / lock-screen
controls) is a *separate, additive* Android feature from the TextToSpeech handoff,
and it can be bolted onto Wcast without deep refactoring to the TTS pipeline.
It is NOT "handing off to the TTS service" in any way.

How it works on Android:
- A media player shows up in the notification shade because an app advertises a
  `MediaSession` (or, with the modern AndroidX Media3 stack, a
  `MediaSessionService` + `MediaSession`). You feed the session a `PlaybackState`
  (playing / paused, position, buffered range, and the set of *supported actions*
  like play/pause/skip-to-next/skip-to-previous/stop/seek) plus `MediaMetadata`
  (title, artist, artwork) and a `MediaStyle` notification.
- The system then renders the controls itself (the compact notification and the
  expanded one with the seek bar + buttons) and routes hardware/media-key input
  and Android Auto/Bluetooth commands back to your session callback.
- The reason Marmalade TTS shows a drawer player is simply that Marmalade (like
  Spotify) *also* builds and publishes its own MediaSession for its audio. It has
  nothing to do with the platform TTS service; it's just an app choosing to expose
  media controls.

Relationship to what Wcast does today:
- Wcast currently talks to the platform `TextToSpeech` engine (which routes to
  the user's chosen engine such as Marmalade) and manages its own playback state
  in `MainViewModel` / `SpeechController` (enqueue, current index, pause/stop,
  skip forward/back).
- A MediaSession would *reflect and control* that same state, not take over
  synthesis. Concretely: when Wcast starts/advances/pauses/stops speech, it would
  also update the MediaSession's PlaybackState; when the user taps the media
  notification's play/pause/skip, the MediaSession callback would invoke the
  exact same `MainViewModel.togglePause()` / `skipBack()` / `skipForward()` /
  `stopSpeaking()` methods the in-app PlayerBar already calls.

What's involved (in rough order of effort):
1. Add the Media3 dependency (`androidx.media3:media3-session`) — modest size;
   conflicts with the project's "keep dependencies minimal / APK small" goal, so
   worth weighing. (A framework-only `MediaSession`/`MediaSessionService` — the
   MediaBrowserServiceCompat path — is also possible but older/more verbose.)
2. A foreground `MediaSessionService` that owns the session and posts the
   `MediaStyle` notification; because playback happens through TTS on the
   main process, the service doesn't have to *contain* the player — it just has
   to own the session and keep the notification alive.
3. A thin bridge updating the session from the existing speaking-state /
   active-position flows and translating media-button callbacks into the
   existing ViewModel calls. Almost no changes to the TTS enqueueing itself.

Open questions / decisions for the human:
- Do you want the media notification to also expose a seek bar with progress?
  TTS doesn't give reliable per-utterance byte/timeline positions, so a
  meaningful seek bar needs per-paragraph (or synthesized-time) position
  estimates — the same position-tracking work already open in ticket 06. A basic
  bar-less set (play/pause + previous/next + stop) is easy; a real seek bar is not.
- The "stop" button in a media session is a *transport stop* (end and clear),
  matching what the player bar's stop already does. That maps cleanly.
- Adding Media3 (or the older support lib) is the main dependency-size tradeoff;
  I'd lean Media3 `media3-session` since it's the supported path and handles the
  notification/service plumbing for us.

No code was changed for this ticket. Recommend we implement it as a modest,
follow-up feature once you confirm the two decisions above (dependency budget and
whether a seek bar is in scope).
