IMPLEMENTED & CLOSED. The app now shows a media-style notification (drawer /
lock-screen player) whenever it is speaking, with transport controls for
previous / play-pause / next / stop, plus a "Paused"/"Now playing" subtitle and
the article title. Tapping the notification reopens the app.

Implementation choice: I used the existing framework `MediaSession` in
`MediaButtonHandler` (rather than adding the Media3 dependency) because Media3's
`MediaSession` is tightly coupled to a `Player` (ExoPlayer) and would require
a dummy player to drive a TextToSpeech stream — awkward and heavier than needed.
A MediaStyle notification (platform `Notification.MediaStyle` + the session
token) gives us the drawer player with stop/prev/next with no new dependency and
minimal APK growth, which fits the project's "keep it small" goal.

Controls: each button is a broadcast to a new `MediaControlReceiver`, routed to
`MediaButtonHandler` -> `MainViewModel` (stop->stopSpeaking, prev->skipBack,
next->skipForward, play-pause->togglePause). This ALSO addresses ticket 09
(media buttons/drawer stop being ignored), since the drawer stop now has a
direct, reliable path. The existing headset main-button path is unchanged.

No seek bar (per your note that none is needed). The notification is dismissed
on stop.

Files: `MediaButtonHandler.kt` (notification + channel + callbacks),
`MediaControlReceiver.kt` (new), `MainViewModel.kt` (wiring + titles),
`AndroidManifest.xml` (receiver + POST_NOTIFICATIONS), `ic_notification.xml`
(new drawable).

Verified: `./gradlew assembleDebug` and `testDebugUnitTest` pass.
One thing to confirm on-device: on Android 13+ the system may prompt for
notification permission; if denied, the media buttons still work but the
notification won't show.
